3|0@progun^1440926759#cuklqvy:Sturmgewehr90$:I'm not saying that isn't true. I'm just trying to point out that generalizing an entire population just won't do anything for us. Sure, there are plenty of Californians who have inherited anti-gun culture. I was that way as well when I was younger. Story time; feel free to skip. My parents, though mostly my mother, have a severe dislike for firearms. I would assume this comes from the fact that they grew up in Vietnam in the 60's-70's and came to the United States as refugees. My mother doesn't talk about it much, but from what I could get out of her, her experience with guns and grenades is not pleasant. They have a reason for being anti-gun, but have enough common decency to understand that America is a different country. Although they raised me to be afraid of guns, they couldn't care less that I eventually changed my mind. Regardless, I guess I'd be in the minority. But at least a minority is better than nothing. Still, for the fact that we continue to love our rights despite the others who would do without, saying that it's my fault we can't have 10+ rd mags, need bullet buttons on our rifles, have an effective handgun ban, and are required to register our firearms does not contribute a thing. But you're right. Ignorance, although unavoidable, is a common occurrence that is inherited by every generation of civilization. Shit~~'s on fire~~ sucks, yo.
